\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{media/media/fig_lasso_path.png}
\caption{LASSO regularization path. Each coloured line shows how a
coefficient evolves as the penalty decreases (right). The dashed vertical
line marks the cross-validation-selected $\alpha$.}
\end{figure}


\hypertarget{decision-trees}{%
\subsection{Decision trees}\label{decision-trees}}

\hypertarget{vanilla-decision-tree}{%
\subsubsection{Vanilla decision tree}\label{vanilla-decision-tree}}


The optimal tree depth selected via validation-set $R^2$ grid search is
\textbf{3} (validation $R^2 = 0.0032$).
The most important splitting feature is \textbf{Momentum 12m}.

The vanilla decision tree model achieves an out-of-sample $R^2$ of -0.0095 on the test set (January 2022 -- October 2024), with RMSE of 0.1860, MAE of 0.1083, and Spearman rank correlation of -0.0469 ($p$-value 0.0000).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{media/media/fig_tree_structure.png}
\caption{Vanilla decision tree structure (best depth = 3,
rendered at depth $\leq 3$ for readability). Shading intensity
indicates the predicted value in each leaf.}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{media/media/fig_tree_importance.png}
\caption{Vanilla decision tree feature importances (mean decrease in
impurity). Only features assigned non-zero importance are split upon.}
\end{figure}


\hypertarget{random-forest}{%
\subsubsection{Random forest}\label{random-forest}}


Grid search over \{\texttt{n\_estimators} \in \{100, 200\},
\texttt{max\_depth} \in \{5, 10\},
\texttt{max\_features} \in \{\text{sqrt}, 0.5\}\} (8 combinations)
selects \textbf{200 trees, max depth 10,
max features sqrt} (validation $R^2 = 0.0054$).
The ensemble averages over many decorrelated trees, reducing variance
relative to the single decision tree.

The random forest model achieves an out-of-sample $R^2$ of -0.0090 on the test set (January 2022 -- October 2024), with RMSE of 0.1860, MAE of 0.1089, and Spearman rank correlation of -0.0278 ($p$-value 0.0000).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.80\linewidth]{media/media/fig_rf_importance.png}
\caption{Random forest feature importances (mean decrease in impurity,
top 15 features). Values are normalised to sum to 1 across all features.}
\end{figure}


\hypertarget{gradient-boosting}{%
\subsubsection{Gradient boosting}\label{gradient-boosting}}


XGBoost is configured with \texttt{n\_estimators=1000},
\texttt{learning\_rate=0.05}, \texttt{max\_depth=5},
\texttt{subsample=0.8}, \texttt{colsample\_bytree=0.8}, and
early stopping with patience 50 evaluated on the validation set.
Training halted at iteration \textbf{79}.

The XGBoost gradient boosting model achieves an out-of-sample $R^2$ of -0.0041 on the test set (January 2022 -- October 2024), with RMSE of 0.1855, MAE of 0.1084, and Spearman rank correlation of 0.0014 ($p$-value 0.6047).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.80\linewidth]{media/media/fig_xgb_importance.png}
\caption{XGBoost feature importances measured by gain (total improvement
in the loss function attributed to each feature across all splits).
Features absent from the figure received zero gain during training.}
\end{figure}


\hypertarget{neural-networks}{%
\subsection{Neural networks}\label{neural-networks}}


The neural network architecture is a 3-layer multi-layer perceptron
(MLP) with hidden layers of sizes $(128, 64, 32)$, ReLU activations,
Adam optimiser ($\eta = 0.001$), and L2 weight decay $\alpha = 0.001$
(12,417 trainable parameters total). Input features are
standardised using a \texttt{StandardScaler} fitted on the training set.
Early stopping monitors validation loss with patience 20; training
stopped at epoch \textbf{30}.

The neural network model achieves an out-of-sample $R^2$ of -0.0059 on the test set (January 2022 -- October 2024), with RMSE of 0.1857, MAE of 0.1087, and Spearman rank correlation of -0.0027 ($p$-value 0.3041).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.82\linewidth]{media/media/fig_nn_loss.png}
\caption{Neural network training and validation loss curves. The dashed
vertical line (where present) marks the early-stopping epoch.}
\end{figure}


\hypertarget{reconciliation-of-results-based-upon-different-estimation-techniques}{%
\subsection{Reconciliation of results based upon different estimation
techniques}\label{reconciliation-of-results-based-upon-different-estimation-techniques}}


Table~\ref{tab:reconciliation} presents the top-10 predicted
industry-adjusted return rankings for November 2024 across all six
estimation methods. Panel A reports the stock ticker at each rank;
Panel B reports the corresponding predicted industry-adjusted return
expressed as a percentage.

Test-set performance varies across methods: OLS: $R^2=-0.001$ | LASSO: $R^2=-0.001$ | VANILLA TREE: $R^2=-0.009$ | RANDOM FOREST: $R^2=-0.009$ | GRADIENT BOOSTING: $R^2=-0.004$ | NEURAL NETWORK: $R^2=-0.006$.
Despite this variation, the rankings show meaningful consistency for
the top-ranked stocks. \textbf{NAII (NATURAL ALTERNATIVES INTL INC)}
appears in the top 3 under 2 of 6 models and is our
recommended buy for November 2024.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.90\linewidth]{media/media/fig_sector_predictions.png}
\caption{Average rank across six models for all 38 Household \& Personal
Products stocks in November 2024. Lower average rank indicates a more
consistent buy recommendation. Crimson bars denote the top-3 consensus picks.}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.90\linewidth]{media/media/fig_metrics_comparison.png}
\caption{Out-of-sample $R^2$ and RMSE for each model evaluated on the
test set (January 2022 -- October 2024).}
\end{figure}


