\hypertarget{appendix-description-of-each-variable}{%
\section{Appendix: Description of each
variable}\label{appendix-description-of-each-variable}}

\hypertarget{real-market-capitalization-and-its-natural-logarithm}{%
\subparagraph{Real market capitalization and its natural
logarithm}\label{real-market-capitalization-and-its-natural-logarithm}}

Real market capitalization is the market value of the stock, adjusted to
purchasing power of November 2025 using the Consumer Price Index. There
is a substantial body of evidence that small stocks have historically
earned higher returns than large stocks (for example, Fama and French,
1992). This is most likely because small stocks are riskier than large
stocks. Compared to companies with market capitalization, small
capitalization companies are likely to have less analyst coverage, less
chance their debt is rated by ratings agencies like S\&P, Moody's and
Fitch, less institutional investment and lower liquidity. Market
capitalization is compiled from the CRSP database accessed via WRDS. The
natural logarithm of real market capitalization is simply a
transformation of real market capitalization for regression analysis to
allow for a more realistic linear relationship between industry-adjusted
stock returns and the market value of equity. Stocks listed in North
America, and indeed most equity markets, have a small percentage of very
large companies and a high percentage of medium and small companies.

\hypertarget{book-to-market-equity}{%
\subparagraph{Book-to-market equity}\label{book-to-market-equity}}

The book-to-market equity ratio is the ratio of the company's book value
of equity from the balance sheet to its market capitalization. There is
a substantial body of evidence that stocks with high book-to-market
ratios have historically earned higher returns than stocks with low
book-to-market ratios (or example, Fama and French, 1992). Whether this
result is due to mispricing of increased risk exposure is a subject of
debate, but prior to the last two decades there was no disputing the
empirical result. Data from the last 20 years suggests that the
book-to-market equity ratio has not been as useful a signal of high
future returns as in the past. This could be due to increased market
efficiency, or a diminished relevance of the book value of equity as a
proxy for the equity holders' investment base. In recent years, there
has been an increase in research and development spending and most
expenditure on R\&D is expensed rather than being capitalized on the
balance sheet and therefore flowing to equity value. The book-to-market
equity ratio was obtained from the Financial Ratios Suite of WRDS.

